          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>This spring, John Koon traveled up Elk Mountain Scenic Highway, a winding road north of Asheville, North Carolina, after word spread among local birders about a rare arrival. Three pairs of red-headed woodpeckers had been spotted among standing snags, and one pair had built what was believed to be the area’s first nest in roughly a decade.</p><p><br></p><p>With some patience and a bit of luck, John caught a glimpse. </p><p><br></p><p>“It’s the stereotypical woodpecker, with a completely red head and stunning black-and-white plumage on the rest of its body,” said John, president of the Blue Ridge Audubon. “It’s really a handsome bird.”</p><p><br></p><p>While fairly common in the open lowlands of the eastern United States, the woodpecker had become a rare sight in the densely wooded mountains around Asheville. That is, until Hurricane Helene tore through Western North Carolina in September 2024, leaving once-dense forests broken and exposed to the sky. All told, an estimated 822,000 acres of timberland were damaged in the state.</p><p><br></p><p>For red-headed woodpeckers, however, that destruction created opportunity. The species prefers open woodlands with dead and decaying trees, which provide insects to eat and cavities for nesting.</p><p><br></p><p>“Even the trees that had been blown down — trees we think of as worthless because they’re on the ground — are still beneficial to woodpeckers,” John said.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

