          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>Elsewhere in Western North Carolina, researchers are beginning to trace how birdlife has shifted since the storm. Clemson University graduate student Adrianna Nelson spent the summer surveying birds in forests largely spared by Helene, forests damaged by it, and damaged areas later managed through practices such as salvage logging. Her work is part of a larger U.S. Forest Service-funded project.</p><p><br></p><p>Although Nelson is still processing her data, one pattern immediately stood out: Her team captured more birds and a wider variety of species in forests altered by Helene.</p><p><br></p><p>The answer is literally in the weeds. A closed canopy leaves little room for the thick, low-growing cover many birds need. When trees fall, sunlight reaches the ground, and shrubs, grasses, and young saplings spring up in the openings, bringing insects, seeds, and plenty of hiding places with them.</p><p><br></p><p>That tangled understory can be especially useful for fledglings still learning to find food and avoid predators. It may also create much-needed habitat for two specific species. The number of ruffed grouse have been dropping in the area for decades. These birds thrive in dense, young forests, which will soon be forming in areas damaged by Helene. The second species is also considered threatened: the golden-winged warbler. It relies on shrubby openings and forest edges, which now abound in Western N.C.</p><p><br></p><p>Still, a busier forest is not necessarily a healthier one. Habitat may attract plenty of birds while providing too little food, too many predators, or too few safe places to raise young. Ecologists call this an ecological sink: Birds move in, but not enough of their young survive to sustain the population.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

