          >
            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>To see beyond immediate beak count, Adrianna’s team used a monitoring protocol known as MAPS, short for Monitoring Avian Productivity and Survivorship. Researchers stretch fine mist nets through the forest and wait for birds to fly into them. Think of it as a nearly invisible tollbooth for feathered friends.</p><p><br></p><p>Each bird is carefully removed, identified, measured, and banded before being released. Researchers record its age and sex, along with details such as muscle development and fat stores. Those measurements can reveal whether young birds in disturbed forests are in better or worse condition than those growing up beneath an intact canopy.</p><p><br></p><p>Clear answers about reproduction and survival may require at least five years of data. The findings could eventually help the Forest Service decide how to manage damaged areas for species at risk. Decisions about where to leave fallen trees and where to remove them could shape which birds benefit from a disturbed forest’s next chapter.</p><p><br></p><p>“In a way, Hurricane Helene was one giant experiment,” Adrianna said. “Now, we can learn how bird species are responding on a really big scale.”</p><p><br></p><p>While we wait for the results of that experiment, the red-headed woodpecker offers an early reminder: A damaged forest is not an empty one.</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
          </div>
        

